/* Problem : Special Positions In a Binary Matrix
Platform:Leetcode
Difficulty:Easy
Link: https://leetcode.com/problems/special-positions-in-a-binary-matrix/description/

//Intuition
A special position in a binary matrix is a cell (i, j) that contains 1 and is the only 1 in its row and its column.
Think of it like this:
If a 1 exists in a row that contains multiple 1s, it cannot be special.
Similarly, if a column has more than one 1, none of those can be special.
So the idea is simple:
Count how many 1s exist in each row and each column.
A cell (i, j) is special if:
mat[i][j] == 1
row_count[i] == 1
col_count[j] == 1
This approach avoids repeatedly scanning rows and columns, which keeps the solution efficient.


//Approach
Step 1: Count 1s in Rows and Columns
Traverse the matrix and maintain:
row_count[i] → number of 1s in row i
col_count[j] → number of 1s in column j
Step 2: Identify Special Positions
Traverse the matrix again and check:
If the cell is 1
If its row and column both contain exactly one 1
If both conditions hold, it is a special position.


//Code
class Solution {
public:
    int numSpecial(vector<vector<int>>& mat) {
             int m=mat.size();
             int n=mat[0].size();
             vector<int>row_count(m,0);
             vector<int>col_count(n,0);
             for(int i=0;i<m;i++)
             {
                for(int j=0;j<n;j++)
                {
                    if(mat[i][j]==1)
                    {
                        row_count[i]++;
                        col_count[j]++;
                    }
                }
             }
             int result=0;
             for(int i=0;i<m;i++)
             {
                for(int j=0;j<n;j++)
                {
                    if(mat[i][j]==0)
                    {
                        continue;
                    }
                    if(row_count[i]==1 && col_count[j]==1)
                    {
                        result++;
                    }
                }
             }
             return result;
    }
};

//Complexity
Time complexity:
First traversal → O(m × n)
Second traversal → O(m × n)
Total:
O(m×n)


Space Complexity
We store two arrays:
row_count → size m
col_count → size n
O(m+n)
